  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186198380,
      "id": "f322cca7-7530-50bf-b262-89faa81f5906",
      "title": "Evaluating Flux Through a Surface"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b3697ff3-f118-507a-a94b-dd9e0df99241",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}\u27e8z,\\,x,\\,y\u27e9\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=\u27e82 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v\u27e9\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 4}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{\u2032}\\times {r}_{v}^{\u2032}=\u27e8-2,\\,3,\\,1\u27e9.$*",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={\u222c}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{\u2032}\\times {r}_{v}^{\u2032})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{\u2032}\\times {r}_{v}^{\u2032}=\u27e8-2,\\,3,\\,1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=\u27e8u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v\u27e9\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{\u2032}\\times {r}_{v}^{\u2032}:$\n\n$$\n\u27e8u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-2,\\,3,\\,1\u27e9=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=2}^{v=4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(10 u + 42)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[u \\left(5 u + 42\\right)]_{2}^{4} \\\\ & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
